Chapter 13: Shortest Paths

Finding shortest path between two nodes of a graph is an important problem that has many practical applications.

Ex: to find shortest possible length between two cities.

In an unweighted graph, the length of a path equals the number of its edges, and we simply use BFS to find shortest path.

Weighted graph sophisticated algorithms are needed to find shortest paths.

------------------------
Bellman - Ford algorithm

Bellman - Ford algorithm finds shortest paths from a starting node to all nodes of the graph.
Algorithm can process all kinds of graph, provided that the graph does not contain a cycle with negative length.
If the graph contains a negative cycle, the algorithm can detect this.

A negative cycle is a cycle that has the sum of all lengths that is negative

The algorithm keeps track of distances from the starting node to all nodes of the graph.
Initially, the distance of the starting node is 0 and the distance to all other nodes in infinite. The algorithm reduces the distance by finding edges that shorten the paths until it is not possible to reduce any distance.

