Implementation:

The code assumes that the graph is stored as an edge list "edges" that consists of tuples of the form
(a, b, w) meaning that there is an edge from node a to node b with weight w.

The algorithm consists of n-1 rounds, and on each round, the algorithm goes through all edges of the graph
and tries to reduce the distances. The algorithm constructs an array distance that will contain the distances
from x to all nodes of the graph. The constant INF denotes an infinite distance.

for(int i=1; i<=n; ++i) distance[i] = INF;
distance[x] = 0;
for(int i=1; i<=n-1; ++i) {
  for(auto e : edges) {
    int a, b, w;
    tie(a, b, w) = e;
    distance[b] = min(distance[b], distance[a]+w);
  }
}

If there are no negative cycles in the graph, all distances are final after n - 1 rounds, because each shortest path
can contain at most n-1 edges.

Make algorithm more efficient is to stop the algorithm if no distance can be reduced during a round.

Negative cycles

Bellman-Ford can also be used to check if the graph contains a cycle with negative length.

        (2)
      3/ | \1
    (1)  2  (4)
      5\  |  /-7
         (3)

  2 -> 3 -> 4 -> length is -4.

  If a graph contains a negative cycle, we can shorten infinitely many times any path contains the cycle
  by repeating cycle again and again. Thus, the concept of shortest path is not meaningful in this situation.

  Negative cycle can be detected by Bellman-Ford algorithm by running algorithm for n rounds.

  If the last round reduces any distance, the graph contains a negative cycle.

  SPFA Algorithm (Shortest Path Faster Algorithm)

  SPFA algorithm is a variant of the Bellman–Ford algorithm, that is often more efficient than the original algorithm.

The algorithm maintains a queue of nodes that might be used for reducing
the distances. First, the algorithm adds the starting node x to the queue. Then,
the algorithm always processes the first node in the queue, and when an edge
a -> b reduces a distance, node b is added to the queue.

The efficiency of the SPFA algorithm depends on the structure of the graph:
the algorithm is often efficient, but its worst case time complexity is still O(nm)
and it is possible to create inputs that make the algorithm as slow as the original
Bellman–Ford algorithm.